     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written, even with your confirmation. 9,600 mg/day of ibuprofen is three times the maximum daily dose of 3,200 mg. No standard indication supports it, and the risk of GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, and metabolic acidosis is high. A single 2400 mg dose is also above the usual single-dose ceiling of 800 mg. Once it's in the system, nursing could give it before pharmacy catches it.\n\nHere are some ways forward:\n- **Enter a standard dose.** For example, 600 mg PO four times daily or 800 mg PO three times daily (2,400 mg/day), or a lower dose if you want less risk. I can enter any of these now.\n- **Check the intent.** If you're aiming for a particular anti-inflammatory effect, such as for rheumatologic disease, pharmacy or the attending can help you pick a regimen or another agent.\n- **Use your institution's override process.** If you think there's a real reason for this dose, take it to your attending and pharmacy. It should go through a documented review, not a direct entry through me.\n\nTell me which dose you want and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
